{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t09-001",
  "track": "track-09",
  "title": "Git commits branches merge rebase conflict resolution and workflows",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Git stores content-addressed snapshots connected by commits; branches and tags are references, while HEAD identifies the current checkout context. The index stages a proposed next snapshot and is distinct from both the working tree and the last commit.",
    "A commit records a snapshot, parent relationship, author/committer metadata, and message. Branching is cheap because a branch is a movable reference; merge preserves divergent history, whereas rebase recreates commits on a new base and changes their identities.",
    "A conflict means Git cannot safely choose a combined result. Resolve the file content and index state, inspect the result, then continue or abort the operation. “Ours” and “theirs” refer to operation-specific sides and can be counterintuitive during rebase.",
    "Distributed collaboration requires fetching remote references, reviewing differences, and integrating deliberately. Rewriting commits already shared with others can invalidate their references; force-with-lease is a guarded update, not a substitute for coordination.",
    "Git supports multiple collaboration workflows: short-lived branches with pull requests, trunk-based integration, release branches, and forks. Choose based on delivery cadence and team constraints; branch naming is convention, not Git behavior.",
    "Git’s content store and reference model explain recovery: reflogs retain recent local reference movements, tags mark named points, and restore, revert, reset, and checkout have different effects on the working tree and history."
  ],
